{{also|double-whammy}}
==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|double-whammy}}

===Etymology===
{{compound|en|double|whammy}}

The phrase is thought to have been popularized by [[cartoonist]] [[w:Al Capp|Al Capp]] (1909&ndash;1979), in his classic [[comic strip]] [[w:Li'l Abner|''Li'l Abner'']].

===Noun===
{{en-noun|double whammies}}

# {{lb|en|informal}} a [[twofold]] [[blow]] or [[setback]]; a series of two events that cause adverse effects
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Robert Kitson|title=England into World Cup semi-finals after bruising victory over Australia|newspaper={{w|The Guardian}}|location=London|publisher=[[w:Guardian Media Group|Guardian News & Media]]|date=19 October 2019|passage=Australia had looked the brighter side initially, probing for gaps in England’s defensive spacing and taking an early lead via Christian Lealiifano’s first successful penalty. Their opponents took a while to make any kind of consistent front-foot impression before May’s '''double whammy''' inside four minutes banished their jangling nerves.}}
# {{lb|en|by extension|informal}} a twofold [[boon]]; a series of two events that cause positive effects

